11/Aug/25 3:19 AM;557058:baa5f297-c116-4036-bbf1-109f8a7e50a7;The help text/error messages fail to mention dashes as allowed in property names. ❌ 

The custom property name in the definition is lowercased by the sanitizer (but not where it’s used), meaning the following doesn’t work because {{NEWcolor}} turns into {{newcolor}} in the root element, but where it’s used it keeps the capital letters:

{noformat}:root {
  --NEWcolor: red;
}

body {
  color: var(--NEWcolor);
}{noformat}

This is confusing. It may be easiest to handle this by disallowing capital letters in the definition since they can’t be used anyway. ❓ 

1 a: Results in the error message when trying to use different symbols like {{|}} ✅ 

2 a: Results in the error messages for {{:root {  --test: 'red';  --test-two: "green"; }}} ✅  

2b: Results in the error message that url is disallowed ✅ 

3 a and b: Results in errors trying to use vars for content and font-family ✅ 

4: Successfully used {{:root { --thick: 20px; } textarea { border: var(--thick) solid black; }}} ✅ 

5: Trying to use fallbacks like {{body {  background-color: var(--best-color, red);  border-color: var(--best-color, var(--okay-color)); }}} results in errors for all of them ✅ 

Work skins:

# Property definition with name {{--background}} and name {{--new-color}} gives the expected error ✅ 
# Property definition with invalid name gives error about invalid name ✅ 

For step 3, the following steps allow me to use a variable:

Attempt to create workskin with following CSS:

{noformat}body {
  color: var(--NEWcolor);
  background-color: var(--NEWcölor);
}{noformat}

This shows as the following after it errors:

{noformat}#workskin body {
  color: var(--NEWcolor);
}{noformat}

Pressing “Update” will save this disallowed use of the var. ❌

