column133:
25/May/26 12:47 PM;557058:48d6a38e-168b-4aa7-a58d-a1853a844295;[~accountid:60d5d58e8f2cc10069d7e879] regarding the first set of questions:

{quote}Should we add a step before step 1 and tell the user to set up an authenticator app on their phone? eg. give them specific instructions for both Android and iOS.{quote}

We want to use the instructions from the admin page, with references to being an admin removed. We do not want to recommend specific app(s) to users, and the experience varies widely across apps (not to mention the issue of updates potentially changing things and making the instructions more confusing).

{quote}I think we should add a hint saying “Scan with your authenticator app” under the QR code. I have a feeling some users will use their camera app otherwise.{quote}

Fair point (I checked my phone, and it wanted to send an SMS for some reason; I was hoping it would do the reasonable thing and offer to open my 2fa app, but no such luck). We can use a footnote, like the “spaces and capitalization don’t matter” hint.

{quote}Should we make it more clear that when they re-generate their backup codes, the old copies will stop working?{quote}

Do you have any suggestions on where/the language to use? Unless it’s a 1-line change, I lean towards “not in this PR, we can see in what ways this comes through to Support and follow up if needed” here.

{quote}Should we automatically disable 2FA whenever a recovery code is used?{quote}

We _can_, but similar to the previous one (this will be a theme), if it will make the PR more than 1k lines of code, we need to defer it to a follow-up PR (which can be shortly after the second, even a draft targeting the existing PR before it’s merged would be fine).



And for the security questions:

{quote}Should we send emails to notify users when they set up or disable 2FA?{quote}

Yes, please! Same goes, though, that if this ends up being a decent amount more code, we need to do it as part of a new PR to make review go more smoothly.

{quote}For password reset requests, should we allow users to reset their password without putting in their 2FA code? I know the current plan is to allow this, but I think this is a bit unconventional.{quote}

This may be addressed by a follow-up ticket later, but not in this PR/ticket. (Also fwiw, Mastodon didn’t do this either last I checked, and they use the same library we do – which makes it annoyingly non-straightforward to do that.)

{quote}Should we allow users to change their email address/password/disable 2FA/re-generate backup codes/delete their account while logged-in without putting in their 2FA code?{quote}

For the initial version, we do not want to require 2fa for these. Besides the already-mentioned review issue, we want to make sure bugs have a chance to shake loose before it requires a wait/Support ticket to fix 2fa (we had 1 report of a problem from AO3 admin accounts, but it was not easily reproducible).

{quote}I guess these would depend on the threat model. Is the threat model to prevent password leaks on other sites leading to account loss (ie. blocking at logins only) or something a bit more advanced, like a sibling or parent with access to a shared computer?{quote}

We generally lean towards the first, as it’s a wider concern (and the second can be partially addressed by using incognito mode or logging out after use). The second case is not _not_ a concern, but not a “must be solved otherwise this feature isn’t useful at all”.

